course: everyday
lesson: 220
title: Bleach and cleaning products
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some people repeat a myth that a small amount of bleach is fine to drink or even good for you; it is not true, because bleach and other cleaning products burn the mouth, throat and stomach.
explain: Cleaning products are made to kill germs and dissolve dirt, and they do the same harm to the soft inside of your body.
explain: They stay in their own labelled bottles, high up or locked away, and different cleaners are never mixed, because some mixtures, like bleach with other cleaners, give off poisonous gas.
explain: If someone swallows a cleaning product, you tell a grown-up at once, who calls the emergency number or the poison helpline; nobody is made to be sick, because the product would burn the throat again on the way up.
explain: A girl in Kansas hears in a video that a drop of bleach in water cures colds; her mum explains that it is a dangerous myth, and they report the video.
fact: Bleach and other cleaning products must never be drunk, not even a small amount.
fact: Cleaning products burn the mouth, throat and stomach.
fact: The idea that a little bleach is safe or healthy to drink is a myth.
fact: Cleaning products are kept in their own labelled bottles.
fact: Cleaning products are stored high up or locked away from small children.
fact: Different cleaning products are never mixed, because some mixtures give off poisonous gas.
fact: If someone swallows a cleaning product, a grown-up calls the emergency number or the poison helpline at once.
fact: You do not make someone be sick after swallowing a cleaning product, because it burns the throat again.
fact: Warning signs on a bottle, like a hazard symbol, mean the product can harm you.
fact: Washing your hands after helping to clean removes any cleaner left on your skin.
exercise: A friend says "A tiny bit of bleach in water is fine to drink". What is true? | It is a myth; bleach must never be drunk | It is true, as long as the water is very cold | It is true for adults only | It is true if you drink it quickly
exercise: Why must cleaning products never be drunk? | They burn the mouth, throat and stomach | They only taste bad, but they do no real harm | They are only harmful in big amounts | They just make you sleepy
exercise: A video online says bleach cures illnesses. What should you think? | It is a dangerous myth | It must be true because it is online | It works if a doctor is not around | It works for colds but not for flu
exercise: Your mum pours a cleaner into an empty juice bottle. Why is that risky? | Someone could think it is juice and drink it | The cleaner slowly stops working once it is in plastic | Juice bottles are too small | The cleaner will smell like juice
exercise: Where should cleaning products be kept in a home with a toddler? | High up or locked away | Under the sink with no lock | On the kitchen table, in sight | In the toy box, so it's tidy
exercise: You want to make the bathroom extra clean by mixing two cleaners. What do you do? | Never mix them | Mix them in a small cup first | Mix them with hot water | Mix them only if they smell nice
exercise: Why should cleaning products never be mixed? | Some mixtures give off poisonous gas | They become far too weak to clean anything properly | They change colour | They become too thick to pour
exercise: Your little brother drank from the bleach bottle. What do you do? | Tell a grown-up at once, who calls for help | Make him be sick straight away to get it all out | Give him more water and wait | Keep it a secret so he is not in trouble
exercise: Why should a child who swallowed a cleaner not be made to vomit? | The product would burn the throat again | Vomiting makes the cleaner much stronger inside the body | It is only bad manners | It always cures the problem
exercise: A bottle has a warning symbol in a red diamond on it. What does it mean? | The product can harm you | The product is safe for children | The product is a sports drink | The product is out of date
exercise: You helped wipe the kitchen with a spray cleaner. What next? | Wash your hands | Lick your fingers clean | Eat a snack straight away | Rub your tired eyes
exercise: Someone says "Natural-looking cleaners are safe to drink". What is true? | No cleaning product is for drinking | Green bottles with leaves on them can be drunk | Lemon-scented ones are made to be drunk | Only the foamy ones are really dangerous
exercise: Which is the right place for a bottle of bleach? | Its own labelled bottle on a high shelf | A clean water bottle in the fridge, with the lid on tight | A cup on the counter | An open bowl under the sink
test: A boy in Cork sees a post saying a spoon of bleach a day keeps you healthy. What is the truth? | It is false and dangerous, as bleach burns the body | It is true for strong people | It is true if mixed with juice | It is true only in winter
test: What do cleaning products do inside the body? | They burn its soft inside parts | They clean out the germs safely | They pass straight through | They make you stronger
test: A toddler is crawling around the kitchen. Which cupboard needs a lock most? | The one with the cleaning products | The one with the plates and the glasses in it | The one with the cereal | The one with the tea towels
test: Your dad is about to pour bleach and another toilet cleaner into the toilet together. What could you remind him? | Mixing cleaners can make poisonous gas | Two cleaners together will clean twice as well and twice as fast | It is fine if he opens the window | Mixing makes them safe to touch
test: A girl in Adelaide finds her baby sister with an open dishwasher tablet. What should she do? | Tell a grown-up at once, who calls the poison helpline | Wait to see if the baby cries | Wash the baby's mouth with soap | Make the baby be sick
test: Why should cleaners stay in their own labelled bottles? | So nobody mistakes them for a drink | So they last longer and don't lose their strength | So they smell stronger | So they cost less
test: Which of these is a myth? | A small amount of bleach is fine to drink | Bleach can burn your skin if it splashes on you | Bleach is kept away from children | Bleach should not be mixed
test: After cleaning the bathroom, why do you wash your hands? | To remove any cleaner left on your skin | To make the cleaner work better next time you use it | Because cleaners are sticky | To cool your hands down
test: someone online says drinking a little bleach is healthy, true? | No, it's a myth; bleach burns the throat | Yes, a little bit cleans you out from the inside | Yes, if mixed with water | Yes, once a week
test: mixin two cleaners together will make it extra clean | Never mix them, some give off poisonous gas | Yes, double the power | Yes, if you add water | Yes, if you stir slowly
test: lil bro drank some floor cleaner, make him sick? | No, a grown-up calls the emergency number or poison helpline | Yes, make him sick at once | Give him salt water | Wait and see what happens
test: helped clean the bathroom, hands feel weird | Wash your hands to remove the cleaner | Lick them clean, it's only a tiny bit | Rub them on your clothes until it's gone | Leave it, it'll dry
explain: A toddler in Texas drank from a cleaning-spray bottle left under the sink; her mouth and throat were burned and she spent days in hospital.
explain: A woman in England mixed bleach with another toilet cleaner to make it stronger; the gas made her choke and she needed help to breathe.
fact: Cleaning products are one of the most common things small children are poisoned by at home.
fact: Bleach mixed with some other cleaners gives off a poisonous gas, so cleaning products must never be mixed.
test: gonna mix bleach n the other spray to make it super strong | No, mixing can make a poison gas | Yes, it'll be double the clean in half the time | Yes, if the window's open | Only a little bit
test: Why are cleaning products kept high up or locked away? | Small children may drink them and be badly burned | They work better in the dark | So they don't freeze | Parents like tidy floors
test: baby brother licked the dishwasher tablet | Tell an adult now and call poison control or the emergency number | Give him a sweet | Wait until he cries | Wipe his mouth and say nothing
test: did u see that video saying a little bit of bleach in water kills all the germs inside u?? real lesson? | Never drink bleach; even a little burns inside | A small sip is healthy as long as it's well diluted | Only adults can drink it safely | It works if you drink it quickly
test: apparently a lady mixed two cleaners in her bathroom and the gas knocked her out and the whole street was evacuated. what's probably blown up? | The street part; but mixing cleaners can make poison gas | That some mixtures make poisonous gas | That cleaners can be dangerous | That bathrooms get cleaned
test: my cousin says if someone drinks cleaner u make them throw up straight away. right? | No, being sick burns the throat again; call emergency | Yes, make them sick at once | Yes, then give them milk and wait | Yes, and then they'll be fine
exam: Identify the principal reason for which different cleaning products must never be mixed. | Certain mixtures release poisonous gas | Mixed cleaners cease to clean effectively in all cases | Mixtures merely smell unpleasant, no more | Mixing renders the bottles far too full
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the drinking of a small amount of bleach? | It is harmful and burns the mouth, throat and stomach | It is safe and destroys the germs within the body, if diluted | It is safe if mixed with juice | It is healthy if taken once weekly
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice where a cleaning product has been swallowed. | An adult calls the emergency number or poison helpline at once | The person is made to vomit immediately | The person is given more water and left | The matter is ignored if no pain is felt
exam: Ascertain the proper storage of cleaning products in a household with small children. | In labelled original bottles, high up or locked away | In drink bottles on the lowest shelf, where they are easily found | Beneath the sink without a lock | In the refrigerator next to the food
